16. ENTROPY AND INFORMATION-THEORETIC ANALYSIS

 

The documentation uses the Shannon entropy formulation:

 

[ H=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_i\log_2(p_i) ]

 

Entropy is meaningful only relative to:

 



the object being measured;

the representation used;

the probability distribution;

the sampling procedure;

the unit of analysis; and

the interpretation assigned to the resulting value.


 

Consequently, an entropy value of zero or a reported entropy difference of zero cannot, by itself, establish:

 



human authorship;

authenticity of intellectual content;

scientific validity;

moral correctness; or

absence of machine assistance.


 

The statement “ΔH = 0 proves human authorship” is therefore not adopted as a valid inference in this consolidated record.

 



 

17. REPRODUCIBILITY MODEL

 

The supplied documentation proposes:

 

[ R= \frac{\text{Checksum Stability}} {\text{Entropy Variance}} ]

 

When entropy variance equals zero, the expression tends toward an undefined or divergent boundary rather than constituting an established scientific reproducibility score.

 

For rigorous reproducibility assessment, the stronger evidentiary approach is procedural:

 

complete inputs + documented methods + software/environment specification + executable workflow + expected outputs + independent rerun + comparison of results

 

Accordingly, reproducibility is treated here as an empirical property of a documented workflow rather than as a consequence of a mathematical ratio alone.

 



 

