31. VERIFICATION SCOPE AND EVIDENTIARY HIERARCHY

 

The consolidated record distinguishes several evidentiary layers.

 

Layer 1 — File Integrity

 

Can establish whether a file corresponds to a specified cryptographic digest.

 

Layer 2 — Documentary Provenance

 

Can establish relationships among versions, identifiers, filenames, dates, metadata, and documented sources.

 

Layer 3 — Computational Reproducibility

 

Requires sufficient code, inputs, environment information, procedures, and independent reruns.

 

Layer 4 — Empirical Validation

 

Requires appropriate data, measurement design, statistical methodology, uncertainty assessment, and independent evaluation.

 

Layer 5 — Philosophical or Normative Argument

 

Requires textual interpretation, conceptual coherence, historical scholarship, comparative reasoning, and explicit normative premises.

 

Layer 6 — Policy Effectiveness

 

Requires real-world implementation evidence, appropriate counterfactual or comparative designs where relevant, longitudinal observation, and independent evaluation.

 

These layers should not be collapsed into a single “verification score.”

 



 

32. WHAT CRYPTOGRAPHIC VERIFICATION DOES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH

 

Cryptographic verification can establish, subject to correct procedure:

 



hash correspondence;

byte-level consistency with a reference;

detection of subsequent file modification;

reproducibility of a documented digest;

limited evidence concerning digital integrity.


 

Cryptographic verification cannot establish by itself:

 



scientific truth;

philosophical correctness;

historical accuracy;

authorship;

causal validity;

policy effectiveness;

ethical superiority;

governmental legitimacy;

constitutional validity;

universal applicability;

or substantive correctness of a research claim.


 

This boundary is a central methodological principle of the consolidated record.

 



 

