35. CONSOLIDATED COMPUTATIONAL APPENDIX

 

35.1 Cryptographic Digest Generation

 

A generic reproducibility procedure may be represented as:

 

import hashlib
import json

with open("document.pdf", "rb") as f:
    data = f.read()

checksums = {
    "MD5": hashlib.md5(data).hexdigest(),
    "SHA1": hashlib.sha1(data).hexdigest(),
    "SHA256": hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest(),
    "SHA512": hashlib.sha512(data).hexdigest()
}

print(json.dumps(checksums, indent=4))


 

The resulting values can be compared against an independently preserved reference manifest.

 

The comparison establishes file-level correspondence, not substantive validity.

 



 

35.2 Shannon Entropy

 

The general formulation is:

 

[ H(X)=-\sum_i p_i\log_2 p_i ]

 

A reproducible entropy analysis must document:

 



the input object;

the representation;

the unit of analysis;

the probability distribution;

the sampling method; and

the interpretation.


 

An entropy result should not be equated with authorship or truth.

 



 

35.3 Illustrative Reproducibility Function

 

def reproducibility_score(checksum_stability, entropy_variance):
    if entropy_variance == 0:
        return None
    return checksum_stability / entropy_variance


 

Returning None at zero variance is preferable to interpreting mathematical divergence as proof of infinite reproducibility.

 



 

35.4 Integrity Composite Index

 

def integrity_composite_index(ethical_governance,
                              algorithmic_transparency,
                              social_trust):
    return (
        ethical_governance
        + algorithmic_transparency
        + social_trust
    ) / 3


 

This is a proposed analytical construct and requires empirical operationalization before substantive use.

 



 

35.5 Illustrative Dharma–Policy Model

 

