45. CONSOLIDATED SCHOLARLY STATEMENT

 

Reimagining Public Policy in Bharat investigates the possibility of placing selected Indic concepts of duty, order, responsibility, ethical authority, public service, and institutional obligation into structured dialogue with contemporary public-policy and governance systems.

 

The research develops an interdisciplinary architecture linking civilizational political thought with policy-cycle analysis, comparative philosophy, institutional ethics, postcolonial and decolonial inquiry, digital information integrity, computational modelling, metadata interoperability, and research-preservation practices.

 

The Version 5–8 documentation further demonstrates a methodological principle of particular importance to digitally mediated scholarship:

 



Different forms of verification answer different kinds of questions.


 

A checksum can establish file correspondence.

 

A provenance record can document lineage.

 

A reproducible computational workflow can support independent reruns.

 

An empirical dataset can support statistical inference.

 

A philosophical argument can provide normative reasoning.

 

A policy evaluation can provide evidence concerning implementation and outcomes.

 

None of these forms of evidence should automatically be substituted for another.

 

The resulting architecture is therefore best interpreted as an attempt to integrate normative philosophy, institutional analysis, computational experimentation, documentary provenance, and digital research integrity without collapsing their distinct evidentiary functions.

 



 

46. FUTURE EMPIRICAL DEVELOPMENT

 

Further empirical development of the framework would benefit from:

 



